From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Thu, 28 Jul 1994 04:22:30 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-7-238] NanoFlaws?

>pjgehrke@ecst.csuchico.edu (Pat) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-187:
>People like Moravec, Hameroff, Drexler, etc. (and even Feynman I 
>suppose) all seem to start from the concept of silicon based 
>nanotechnology. 

Drexler's designs have mechanical computers made from atomic precision 
diamondoid parts.  They would be much more resistant to EMP than
any microelectronic device.  Due to their atomic precision, they
would be more vulnerable to focused high-energy radiation attacks.  
EMP resistance may even be one of the first applications of nanomechanical 
computers.  In this market niche mechanical computing becomes economical 
even if it's still somewhat slower than electronic logic.

A related point that Drexler et. al. haven't pursued yet
is that nanomechanical computers might achieve reasonable speeds
even without atomic resolution.  Thus we might see near-future
( < 15 years) commercial application with 10-100 nm 3D lithographic 
techniques and "in-place assembly" (lithography produces the
moving mechanisms in place, so no assembly is required).  Already 
Texas Instruments has run chips with millions of moving parts 
(micron-sized mirrors) for several months without a failure.
We can achieve good reliability in mechanical systems 
lacking atomic resolution, as long as they use a small variety 
of well-tested, consistently manufactured parts.  A rod
logic computer requires only a small variety of parts.

