From: jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker)
Date: Thu, 28 Jul 1994 10:00:41 -0800
Subject: [#94-7-240] NanoFlaws?

>>pjgehrke@ecst.csuchico.edu (Pat) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-187:
>>People like Moravec, Hameroff, Drexler, etc. (and even Feynman I 
>>suppose) all seem to start from the concept of silicon based 
>>nanotechnology. 
>
>Drexler's designs have mechanical computers made from atomic precision 
>diamondoid parts.  They would be much more resistant to EMP than
>any microelectronic device.......

There are ways of making conventional electronic devices EMP resistant
without making the circuits themselves invulnerable. The problem with EMP
is that it turns transistors on randomly. This can cause CMOS logic to
destroy itself in milliseconds. 

A hardened B-52 has EMP sensors that shut down all loads within a few
milliseconds. Everything is designed to reboot quickly ( in under 2
seconds) and keep very frequent backups of critical states. I don't know
what kind of device holds the backup data but it has to be unaffected by
EMP. I learned this from a Harris Semiconductor data book that described
EMP sensor IC's.

I hope EMP resistance doesn't become necessary for uploads (except perhaps
during space travel) but if it does there are ways to cope. They
psychological effect would be like "oops; I just lost my train of thought.
Perhaps I got zapped by EMP and rebooted myself to a recently saved state".



